         "position": ""
      },
      {
         "id": "c924d397-9582-4acc-9692-bef3b93e1d30",
         "audio": "",
         "smile": "inlove",
         "source_menu": [
            {
               "type": 1,
               "uuid": "4742a2d6-a3c5-4883-beb5-8ed0240fa9a7",
               "text": "Weisstein, Eric W.",
               "page": "https://mathworld.wolfram.com/LongDivision.html",
               "date_time": {
                  "type": "gregory",
                  "year": "2026",
                  "month": "",
                  "day": ""
               }
            },
            {
               "type": 1,
               "uuid": "e1f0eeb1-c74b-4e7e-97dd-90f592fab221",
               "text": "Original Text",
               "page": "https://www.historytracers.org/index.html?page=class_content&arg=e1f0eeb1-c74b-4e7e-97dd-90f592fab221",
               "date_time": {
                  "type": "gregory",
                  "year": "2026",
                  "month": "",
                  "day": ""
               }
            }
         ],
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "#### Understanding the Steps\n\nTo do the division, we followed these steps:\n\nBecause 3 is smaller than 5, we began by dividing 32 by 5, which gives 6.\n\nThen we multiplied 6 by 5 and subtracted the result from 320. Notice that we added a zero at the end, because we were dividing the hundreds in 32(0), and not just the number 32.\n\nAfter that, we had 20 left, which we divided again, obtaining 4.\n\nFinally, we multiplied 4 by 5, which gave 20 once more, and we performed the final subtraction.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ],
         "desc": null,
